// `r == s` over two references is refused BY NAME: no operator applies to a
// reference, and this one applied to the ADDRESSES.
//
// A reference is a C pointer. The type checker typed `&a` as `a`, so `==` saw
// two `i64`s and code generation emitted a pointer comparison. MEASURED on
// d5166e8 before this refusal: it compiled, ran, exited 0 and printed `ne` for
// `5 == 5` — the silent-wrong-result class. `s - r` printed `-1` for `7 - 5`
// (a pointer difference), `r += 1` stepped the pointer, `!r` and `while r` read
// a pointer's truth (the second never ended). `*r == *s` compares the values.
fn main() {
    let a: i64 = 5;
    let b: i64 = 5;
    let r = &a;
    let s = &b;
    if r == s {
        print("eq");
    } else {
        print("ne");
    }
}
